> Nothing to lose ?!!? Rubbish.

>You can lose a valuable piece of armour via a scroll of destroy armour.

If you don't know what a scroll is, either identify it, or
be more careful reading it.  If I haven't found destroy armor
yet, I will read unidentified (and uncursed scrolls) while
wearing nothing but an orcish helm, etc.   In short, this
case is 100% avoidable.

>You can gain unfortunate things like balls on a chain.

Easiest avoidance is to identify scrolls before reading them.
The Whole point to 'unfortunate things' like this is so that
you have a risk factor when you go out on a limb.  Would it
be preferable if all magic items were beneficial, all levels
instantly mapped, and all monsters harmless?

Besides, there are several ways to counteract the Punishment
scroll (including blessing it).  Also, a rumor says something
about punished people having one of the best non-artifact       .
weapons in the game.

>Accidently stepping in water and saying goodbye spellbooks.

Stepping in water is eminently avoidable, and most certainly
is not the game's fault.  It's one thing to not notice a
rust trap (which doesn't affect spellbooks).  It's another
thing entirely to rush off into a pond.  (If you tried to
run while confused, that's also your own problem).

As far as the original reply regarding Nothing to Lose,
that was in response to the question:
      how do you save games to restore them if you get killed?
The reply was to use Discovery mode because in that case,
you will no longer get killed.  After all, that's all the
original person wanted -- to continue his dead character.

You can learn from mistakes, and continue playing by
using Discovery mode.  Restoring backups to make up for
the avoidable mistakes that were listed above, however,
is something completely different.  If you don't learn
to avoid things like that, you will probably spend more
time copying your backups than playing the game itself.

